radioglobe - internet radio on a spinning globe

DESCRIPTION
	Radioglobe presents an interactive orthographic globe
	with radio station markers. Click a station to start
	streaming. Drag to spin the globe. Scroll to zoom.

	Given an earth file (see EARTH MASK), the globe is drawn
	as a solid, lit sphere texture-mapped from a real
	equirectangular satellite photo (or from a land/ocean mask
	baked out of the coastline data), with bilinear texel
	filtering; the vector coastlines and lat/lon grid are drawn
	on top.  Releasing a drag leaves the globe spinning and it
	eases to a stop, and motion frames are rendered at reduced
	resolution so dragging and spinning stay smooth even with
	the per-pixel textured earth.  Without an earth file the
	rendering is the plain ocean disc plus coastline outlines.

	Inspired by radio.garden, built for 9front.

USAGE
	radioglobe [-s stationfile] [-c coastfile] [-e earthmask] [-r devrate]

	The default station file is /lib/radio/stations and the
	default coastline file is /lib/radio/coast.dat (also
	searched for as ./coast.dat).  Samples can be generated
	with the converters below.

	-e names an earth mask file (see EARTH MASK below); if not
	given, ./earth.mask and then /lib/radio/earth.mask are
	tried.  If none of those exist, radioglobe falls back to
	the flat ocean-disc + coastline-outline globe exactly as
	before -- the earth mask is entirely optional.

	-r sets the audio device sample rate in Hz (default 44100).
	Use -r 48000 if your /dev/audio runs at 48 kHz.  See AUDIO.

	To build everything:
		mk

	radioglobe links libview (/usr/dave/work/libview), the
	shared view-math library that owns the Vec3 helpers, the
	orbit-camera basis (viewbasis()) and the inertial-drag
	state machine (Orbit/orbittick()); claudegraph links the
	same library.  The mkfile rebuilds it from that work tree
	automatically, so plain "mk" here is enough -- there is no
	separate install step to remember.  The converters
	(mkcoast, mkstations, mkearth) do not use it.

	Note: run "mk clean" and "mk" as separate commands.  In one
	invocation mk stats the object files before clean removes
	them, then the link step cannot find them.

	To install the binaries:
		mk install

	To install the data files:
		mk installdata

	To run:
		radioglobe
	or with local data:
		radioglobe -s stations -c coast.dat

DATA

	Coastlines come from Natural Earth (public domain) and
	stations from radio-browser.info (open community database).
	Three converters, built by mk, turn the raw downloads (plus,
	for the earth mask, coast.dat itself) into the formats
	radioglobe loads.

	Coastlines.  Fetch a Natural Earth coastline GeoJSON, e.g.
	the coarse 1:110m theme:

		hget https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector/master/geojson/ne_110m_coastline.geojson > /tmp/coast.geojson
		mkcoast < /tmp/coast.geojson > coast.dat

	For more detail use ne_50m_coastline.geojson instead.

	Stations.  Fetch the most-played geolocated stations:

		hget 'https://de1.api.radio-browser.info/json/stations/search?limit=1000&has_geo_info=true&hidebroken=true&order=clickcount&reverse=true' > /tmp/stations.json
		mkstations < /tmp/stations.json > stations

	mkstations keeps MP3, OGG/Vorbis, and AAC/AAC+ streams by
	default (AAC needs audio/aacdec installed); pass -a to keep
	all codecs and HLS, or -b N to require at least N kbps.
	HLS (.m3u8) playlists are skipped by default since the
	decoders cannot demux them.

	Earth mask or texture (optional).  Bakes an equirectangular
	earth for radioglobe's solid, lit, shaded globe mode (instead
	of the flat ocean disc + coastline outlines), in one of two
	ways:

		mkearth < coast.dat > earth.mask

	bakes a land/ocean mask from an existing coast.dat (flat,
	generated colors), or

		hget https://.../earth.jpg > /tmp/earth.jpg
		jpg -9 -t /tmp/earth.jpg > /tmp/earth.plan9img
		mkearth -i /tmp/earth.plan9img > earth.mask

	converts a real equirectangular photo (e.g. NASA's public-
	domain Blue Marble Next Generation, visibleearth.nasa.gov)
	into an RGB texture instead.  Both write the same earth.mask
	file radioglobe loads via -e; see EARTH MASK below for both
	formats and rendering notes.  Without an earth.mask,
	radioglobe is unchanged -- this is a purely additive
	rendering mode.

CONTROLS
	Mouse:
		Button 1 drag   spin the globe
		Button 2 click  play station under cursor
		Button 3        menu (play/stop/reload/exit)
		Scroll up       zoom in
		Scroll down     zoom out

	Releasing a button-1 drag hands the globe its launch
	velocity: it keeps spinning and eases to a stop under
	friction, like flicking a real globe.  Grabbing it again
	stops it dead.  The spin decays in wall-clock time rather
	than per rendered frame, so a flick lasts the same length
	whether frames are cheap (plain vector globe) or expensive
	(textured earth at full window size).

	Zoom runs from 0.5x to 512x.  Deep zoom exists mostly to
	separate stations packed into one city; stations at exactly
	identical coordinates can never be separated by zooming.

	Keyboard:
		arrows          rotate view
		+/-             zoom in/out
		0               reset view
		space/enter     play selected station
		s or .          stop playback
		q or Del        quit

STATUS BAR
	The strip along the bottom of the window shows, in order of
	precedence: the playing station (and the hovered one after
	a "|" if they differ), or the hovered station with its
	coordinates, or -- when nothing is playing or hovered --
	the view center, the zoom factor, and the cost in
	milliseconds of the previous rendered frame.

	That millisecond figure is the diagnostic to read first if
	interaction ever feels rough: a large number means frames
	are simply expensive (strobing/low frame rate), a small one
	with visible flicker only during motion means presentation
	tearing (nothing left to fix in the application), and
	flicker on a completely idle globe means a stale binary or
	something outside radioglobe.  Hovering stations does not
	recompute it, so if the number changes while you merely
	wave the mouse over dots, the running binary is older than
	the selection-overlay work: mk install.

STATION FILE FORMAT
	Lines of the form:
		lat lon "station name" stream-url
	or:
		lat lon station-name stream-url

	Coordinates are decimal degrees. North latitude and
	east longitude are positive. Lines starting with #
	are comments.

COASTLINE FILE FORMAT
	coast.dat holds one polyline per block.  A header line
		> N
	gives the point count N, followed by N lines of
		lat lon
	in decimal degrees.  Blank lines and lines starting with
	# are ignored.  This is what mkcoast emits.

EARTH MASK
	earth.mask is a baked equirectangular earth, generated
	offline by mkearth, in one of two formats (radioglobe
	auto-detects which one from the file's first line):

	Land/ocean mask, from coast.dat:

		mkearth [-w width] [-t preview.tga] < coast.dat > earth.mask

	-w sets the mask width in pixels (height is always width/2);
	default 2048.  -t writes a preview .tga (land/ocean, unlit)
	alongside the mask for eyeballing the result.  File format:

		EARTHMASK
		w h
		<w*h raw bytes, row-major from the north pole:
		 1=land 0=ocean>

	mkearth rasterizes every coastline segment from coast.dat
	into the grid, then flood-fills ocean from a handful of
	known open-ocean seed points; anything the flood cannot
	reach counts as land.  This means closed seas whose ring is
	present in the data (e.g. the Caspian) come out as land, and
	Antarctica needs no special-casing.  It also means an
	unclosed coastline ring in the source data would leak the
	flood into that landmass and mark it ocean by mistake --
	mkearth prints the resulting land percentage (~30% is
	correct for Earth) as a sanity check; a much lower number
	means a leak, diagnosable with -t.

	Real photo texture, from an equirectangular world image
	(e.g. NASA Blue Marble Next Generation):

		jpg -9 -t earth.jpg > earth.plan9img   # -9: uncompressed image(6); -t: force RGB
		mkearth -i earth.plan9img > earth.mask

	imagefile must be an UNCOMPRESSED Plan 9 image(6) file --
	produce one from a JPEG/PNG/TIFF with jpg/png/tif's -9 -t
	options (see jpg(1)).  No resampling is done: whatever
	resolution you feed in is what radioglobe samples from, so
	pick something reasonable (a few thousand pixels wide is
	plenty; the full-resolution multi-tile Blue Marble releases,
	tens of thousands of pixels wide, are overkill for an
	on-screen globe and slow to load).  Texture samples are
	bilinear-filtered (the land/ocean mask stays nearest-
	neighbor, since its hard block edges are already covered by
	the exact vector coastline drawn on top), so a modest
	resolution still looks smooth even zoomed all the way in --
	you do not need to chase a bigger image to fix blockiness.
	File format:

		EARTHTEX
		w h
		<w*h*3 raw bytes, row-major from the image's own row 0
		 (must be the north pole, as in any standard
		 equirectangular world map): B,G,R per pixel>

	In both cases, when radioglobe finds an earth.mask (via -e,
	or ./earth.mask, or /lib/radio/earth.mask, in that order), it
	switches from the flat ocean-disc rendering to a solid, lit,
	shaded globe: each visible screen pixel is projected back
	onto the sphere (exact for an orthographic view of a single
	sphere -- no mesh, no z-buffer needed) and looked up in the
	mask/texture for its color (land/ocean flat color, or the
	real photo's RGB), then shaded by a fixed viewer-attached
	light.  The vector coastline outlines and lat/lon grid are
	still drawn on top from coast.dat as before, so the visible
	outline stays exact even though the fill underneath it is a
	raster approximation.  While the globe is being dragged or is
	spinning, the shaded fill renders at reduced resolution to
	keep the motion smooth and sharpens automatically the moment
	it stops; the outlines, grid, and station dots stay full
	resolution throughout.  With no earth.mask found, rendering
	is identical to a build with none at all.

AUDIO
	Playback runs this rc pipeline per station:

	    play -o /fd/1 URL </dev/null | \
	        audio/pcmconv -o s16c2r<devrate> >/dev/audio

	play(1) fetches the URL (hget/webfs), detects the format,
	and decodes it (mp3dec/oggdec/aacdec/...), writing raw PCM
	to stdout instead of opening /dev/audio itself.  pcmconv
	then resamples that PCM to the device rate before it reaches
	/dev/audio.

	Why the pcmconv stage: a decoder that emits 48000 Hz (common
	for AAC) written straight to a 44100 Hz /dev/audio plays back
	about 9% too fast ("sped up").  Forcing the rate with pcmconv
	makes decoder and device agree, fixing the pitch/speed.  Set
	-r to match your device if it is not 44100 Hz.

	Finding your device rate: the rate is owned by the audio
	driver and exposed via audio(3).  Read it from /dev/volume:

		grep speed /dev/volume      # e.g. "speed 44100"

	and pass that number to -r.  You can also change the device:

		echo speed 48000 >/dev/volume

	in which case run radioglobe -r 48000 so the two agree.

	For formats not handled by the standard decoders, add a
	decoder to $path; AAC needs audio/aacdec so play(1) can
	invoke it for the *aac* case.

REQUIREMENTS
	webfs(4) must be mounted for HTTP streaming to work.
	Typically this is already running under rio.

	libview must be present at /usr/dave/work/libview; mk
	builds it as part of building radioglobe.

SOURCE
	main.c         event loop, UI, station management, audio,
	               frame pacing and the momentum tick
	globe.c        orthographic projection, rendering, solid
	               earth (land/ocean mask or photo texture),
	               per-pixel shading and station dots
	coast.c        loads coast.dat at runtime, chunks polylines
	dat.h          shared types
	util.h util.c  helpers shared by the converters
	mkcoast.c      GeoJSON coastline -> coast.dat
	mkstations.c   radio-browser JSON -> stations
	mkearth.c      coast.dat -> earth.mask land/ocean raster,
	               or (-i) a Plan 9 image -> RGB earth texture
	NOTES.md       project notes / how to resume work
	CODE-REVIEW.md static review of the whole tree

	Not in this directory:

	libview        /usr/dave/work/libview -- Vec3 math, the
	               orbit-camera view basis and the inertial
	               drag/momentum state machine, shared with
	               claudegraph.  Linked as libview.a$O; see
	               that tree's README.
